>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `harvest_registrations`

``` hol4
Refute.harvest_registrations : unit ->
  {typedefs : hol_type list,
   quotients : hol_type list,
   theories_scanned : string list}
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Sweeps every theory in the current session's `Theory.ancestry`, oldest
first, once, attempting standard typedef and quotient harvesting for
every type operator those theories declare.

This is an opt-in, eager alternative to the model finder's default
demand-driven harvest, which only registers a type's metadata the first
time an unregistered Abs/Rep occurrence for it is encountered while
processing a goal.  `harvest_registrations` reuses that same lazy harvest
for every candidate type operator, so every incompatibility guard, clash
guard, and negative-cache entry behaves identically; it is not a second
harvest implementation, only an eager caller of the existing one.

The returned record lists only what this call newly registered: a type
already registered, whether by an earlier explicit registration or an
earlier harvest, is not listed.  Consequently an immediate second call
returns empty lists, and calling it costs nothing beyond the first sweep
for any type it has already settled, positively or negatively.
`theories_scanned` is the whole swept ancestry, not the narrower,
per-operator set of theories whose theorems the harvest actually
inspected.

### Failure

A type operator that harvesting cannot classify, or that already carries
an incompatible classification, is silently skipped.  This covers only
the per-operator harvesting attempt: enumerating the ancestry and each
theory's type operators runs outside that handler and can still raise.

### See also

[`Refute.register_typedef`](#Refute.register_typedef),
[`Refute.register_quotient`](#Refute.register_quotient),
[`Refute.model_refute`](#Refute.model_refute)

Lazy and whole-ancestry harvesting are described in
\ref{Description:the-holrefute-library}.
